__d(function(e,a,o,t,r,i,s){r.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:7,subject:"Mathematics",textbook:"Mathematics \u2013 Class 7",chapter_number:15,chapter_title:"Visualising Solid Shapes",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How many faces, edges, and vertices does a cube have?",back:"Cube: Faces (F) = 6 (all square). Edges (E) = 12. Vertices (V) = 8. Verification using Euler's formula: F + V \u2212 E = 6 + 8 \u2212 12 = 2 \u2713.",memory_hook:"Cube: 6-8-12. Remember with a story: a die has 6 faces (1 to 6 dots), 8 corners (one per room in a small house), 12 edges (12 months in a year). 6, 8, 12 \u2014 even numbers all, in order!"},{id:"fc02",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"State Euler's Formula. What does each letter stand for?",back:"Euler's Formula: F + V \u2212 E = 2. F = number of Faces (flat surfaces). V = number of Vertices (corner points). E = number of Edges (line segments where faces meet). This formula is true for ALL convex polyhedra \u2014 solids with flat polygonal faces. NOT valid for curved surfaces (cylinder, cone, sphere).",memory_hook:"Euler's Formula = 'Faces + Vertices \u2212 Edges = 2'. Remember: F + V = E + 2. Or think: 'Funny Very Exciting = 2' (FVE=2). Or: 'For Very Elegant polyhedra, always get 2'!"},{id:"fc03",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How many faces, edges, and vertices does a triangular prism have? Verify with Euler's formula.",back:"Triangular prism: F = 5 (2 triangular bases + 3 rectangular lateral faces). V = 6 (3 on top triangle + 3 on bottom triangle). E = 9 (3 top + 3 bottom + 3 vertical). Euler's: F + V \u2212 E = 5 + 6 \u2212 9 = 2 \u2713.",memory_hook:"TRIANGULAR PRISM = 5-6-9. Think 'five six nine' like a phone number. F=5 (two triangles + three sides), V=6 (two sets of 3 triangle corners), E=9 (3+3+3 \u2014 three rows of 3 each)!"},{id:"fc04",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the F, V, and E of a square pyramid? Verify Euler's formula.",back:"Square pyramid: F = 5 (1 square base + 4 triangular faces). V = 5 (4 base corners + 1 apex/tip). E = 8 (4 base edges + 4 lateral edges from base to apex). Euler's: F + V \u2212 E = 5 + 5 \u2212 8 = 2 \u2713.",memory_hook:"SQUARE PYRAMID = 5-5-8. F and V are both 5 \u2014 the square pyramid is the only common solid where F = V! E = 8 (like 8 legs of a spider \u2014 4 base + 4 going up to the tip)."},{id:"fc05",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a net of a solid shape? Give one example.",back:"A net is a flat (2D) arrangement of a solid's faces that, when folded along the connecting edges, forms the 3D solid. Example: Net of a cube \u2014 6 equal squares arranged in a cross (or T, or L, or other valid configurations) that fold into a cube. A cube has 11 different valid nets.",memory_hook:"NET = 'Not Enough Thickness' \u2014 it is FLAT. Fold the flat NET to get the SOLID. Like origami: start flat, fold into 3D. Cardboard box before assembly = NET. Cardboard box after assembly = SOLID CUBOID."},{id:"fc06",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the three standard views used to represent a 3D solid in 2D? Describe each.",back:"Three standard views: 1. Front view (Front elevation): the 2D shape seen when looking directly at the solid from the front. 2. Side view (Side elevation): the 2D shape seen from the left or right side. 3. Top view (Plan view): the 2D shape seen when looking directly down from above. These three views together completely describe any 3D solid for engineering and architectural drawings.",memory_hook:"FST = Front, Side, Top. 'Finding Shapes Thoughtfully' from three directions. Like examining a mystery object in a dark room: look from the front (F), look from the side (S), look from above (T) \u2014 now you know what it is!"},{id:"fc07",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the front view, side view, and top view of a cylinder?",back:"Cylinder views: Front view = Rectangle (width = diameter, height = cylinder height). Side view = Rectangle (same dimensions as front view). Top view = Circle (you look down and see the circular face, diameter = cylinder diameter). Remember: cylinder = tin can. Look at a tin from front/side = rectangle. Look from top = circle.",memory_hook:"CYLINDER: Front/Side = RECTANGLE (the 'tall' view). Top = CIRCLE (the 'looking down' view). Think of a Pringles can: from front you see rectangle, from top you see circle. Simple!"},{id:"fc08",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the three views of a cone?",back:"Cone views: Front view = Triangle (an isosceles triangle \u2014 two slant sides and the base). Side view = Triangle (same as front view). Top view = Circle (looking down from above, you see the circular base with a dot at centre for the apex). Think: ice cream cone \u2014 from front = triangle, from top = circle with point in middle.",memory_hook:"CONE: Front/Side = TRIANGLE (pointy!). Top = CIRCLE with a DOT in the centre (the apex projected). Ice cream cone: front = triangle. Top = circle. Easy to remember because you EAT it from the top (circle) and see the triangle from the front!"},{id:"fc09",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why can't Euler's formula F + V \u2212 E = 2 be applied to a sphere?",back:"Euler's formula applies ONLY to polyhedra \u2014 solids with flat polygonal faces. A sphere has NO flat faces, NO straight edges, and NO vertices. It is a curved surface with F=0 (or technically 1 curved face), V=0, E=0. 0 + 0 \u2212 0 \u2260 2. Cylinders and cones also have curved surfaces, so the formula does not apply to them either.",memory_hook:"Euler's formula loves FLAT faces and STRAIGHT edges \u2014 POLYHEDRA only! Sphere, cylinder, cone have CURVES \u2014 Euler says 'I cannot work with curves!' Remember: POLY (many flat faces) = EULER. CURVES = NO EULER."},{id:"fc10",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Describe the net of a cylinder.",back:"The net of a cylinder consists of three parts: 1. Two circles (one for the top face and one for the bottom face). 2. One rectangle (the lateral/curved surface, which unrolls flat; its length = circumference of the circle = 2\u03c0r, and width = height of cylinder h). When the rectangle is rolled up and the two circles are attached, it forms the cylinder.",memory_hook:"Cylinder net = 2 CIRCLES + 1 RECTANGLE. Think of a tin can: open top and bottom (2 circles) + unroll the body (1 rectangle). Can of paint = cylinder. Unroll the label = rectangle. Remove top and bottom = 2 circles. That's the net!"},{id:"fc11",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A solid has F=8, V=12, E=18. Verify whether it satisfies Euler's formula.",back:"Apply Euler's formula: F + V \u2212 E = 8 + 12 \u2212 18 = 2. Yes! 8 + 12 \u2212 18 = 20 \u2212 18 = 2 \u2713. This solid satisfies Euler's formula, so it is a valid convex polyhedron. (This matches a hexagonal prism: 2 hexagonal bases + 6 rectangular faces = 8 faces; 6+6=12 vertices; 6+6+6=18 edges.)",memory_hook:"To CHECK Euler: just plug in F+V\u2212E. If answer = 2 \u2192 valid polyhedron \u2713. If \u2260 2 \u2192 invalid or non-polyhedron \u2717. It is the EASIEST geometry check in the book \u2014 just add F and V, then subtract E. Should always get 2!"},{id:"fc12",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How many nets does a cube have? Give two examples of valid arrangements.",back:"A cube has exactly 11 different valid nets. Two examples: 1. Cross/plus shape: 4 squares in a column with 1 square on each side of the second square from top (the classic cross). 2. T-shape: 3 squares in a row, with 3 more squares forming a T attached. Invalid arrangement: 4 squares in a straight 1\xd74 line \u2014 this does NOT fold into a cube (opposite faces would overlap).",memory_hook:"11 nets of a cube \u2014 the magic number! Think: '11 ways to unwrap a cube gift box'. The cross shape is the most common. Straight line (1\xd76) = INVALID (remember: 6-in-a-row NEVER works). 11 valid nets, infinite wrong ones!"},{id:"fc13",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the difference between a prism and a pyramid?",back:"Prism: A polyhedron with TWO identical parallel polygonal bases connected by rectangular lateral faces. The bases are the same shape and size, directly above each other. Examples: triangular prism, cuboid (rectangular prism), hexagonal prism. Pyramid: A polyhedron with ONE polygonal base and triangular lateral faces that all meet at a single point (apex). Examples: square pyramid, triangular pyramid (tetrahedron), Egyptian pyramids.",memory_hook:"PRISM = Two bases, PARALLEL, connected by rectangles (like a Toblerone chocolate box \u2014 triangular prism!). PYRAMID = One base, triangular faces pointing UP to a TIP (like the Egyptian pyramids). PRISM: Pair of bases. PYRAMID: Point at top."},{id:"fc14",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"The Gol Gumbaz in Bijapur (Karnataka) has a square base topped with a dome. What solid shapes does this represent?",back:"The Gol Gumbaz's square base is approximately a CUBOID (or cube \u2014 square cross-section). The dome (rounded roof) is a HEMISPHERE (half of a sphere). The complete structure = cuboid + hemisphere. From the front view, you would see a rectangle (cuboid) with a semicircle on top (dome). From the top, you would see a square with a circle in the centre. Gol Gumbaz has one of the world's largest domes, demonstrating hemisphere geometry at an architectural scale.",memory_hook:"Gol Gumbaz = CUBOID body + HEMISPHERE dome. 'GOL' means round in Kannada/Urdu \u2014 the GOL (round/hemisphere) is the Gumbaz (dome)! From outside = rectangle + semicircle. From above = square + circle. Karnataka's greatest geometry lesson!"},{id:"fc15",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What solid shape has the same view (circle) from all three directions?",back:"A sphere has the same view (a circle) from all three standard directions: front view = circle, side view = circle, top view = circle. This makes the sphere the most symmetric solid \u2014 from any direction, it looks like a circle. No other common solid has this property. A hemisphere would show a circle from top (the flat face) but a semicircle from front and side.",memory_hook:"SPHERE = CIRCLE from every direction. No matter where you look \u2014 front, side, top, bottom, diagonal \u2014 a sphere always shows a CIRCLE. It is the 'perfect' solid. Like the sun or a football \u2014 circle from everywhere!"},{id:"fc16",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name the net components of a cone. Describe each part.",back:"A cone's net has two parts: 1. A CIRCLE \u2014 the flat circular base of the cone. 2. A SECTOR (a 'pie slice' shape cut from a larger circle) \u2014 the lateral/curved surface. When the sector is rolled up to form a cone shape and the circle is attached at the bottom, you get the cone. The radius of the sector = slant height of the cone. The arc length of the sector = circumference of the base circle.",memory_hook:"Cone net = CIRCLE (base) + SECTOR (fancy pie slice). Think: ice cream cone (sector) + circular scoop holder (circle). Roll the sector into a pointy cone shape, stick the circle at the bottom. Instant geometry ice cream!"},{id:"fc17",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"An architect draws a floor plan of a building. Which standard view is a floor plan?",back:"A floor plan is the TOP VIEW (also called the plan view) \u2014 what you see when looking directly down at the building from above. It shows the arrangement of rooms, walls, doors, and corridors as seen from a bird's-eye perspective. Architects also draw the front elevation (front view) and side elevation (side view) to fully describe the building. Together, these three views are sufficient for a builder to construct the 3D building.",memory_hook:"FLOOR PLAN = TOP VIEW = Bird's Eye View. Imagine you are a bird flying above the building and looking straight down \u2014 the shapes you see = the floor plan. Architects, ISRO, and BBMP (Bengaluru's city planning body) all use these three views daily."},{id:"fc18",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A solid has 6 faces, 8 vertices, and 12 edges. What two common solid shapes could this be?",back:"F=6, V=8, E=12 satisfies Euler's formula: 6+8\u221212=2 \u2713. Two solids with these counts: 1. Cube \u2014 6 square faces, 8 vertices, 12 edges. 2. Cuboid (rectangular prism) \u2014 6 rectangular faces (pairs of opposite faces equal), 8 vertices, 12 edges. Both cube and cuboid have the SAME F, V, E counts! The shape of the faces distinguishes them (all squares vs different rectangles).",memory_hook:"6-8-12: the MAGIC NUMBERS of cube AND cuboid. Same skeleton (F, V, E) \u2014 different face shapes! Cube = all square faces. Cuboid = rectangular faces. Like identical triplets wearing different outfits!"},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is visualising 3D shapes from 2D drawings an important skill?",back:"Visualising 3D from 2D is essential because: (1) Engineers and architects communicate complex 3D designs using 2D drawings (blueprints, floor plans, elevations). (2) Computer-generated 3D images (movies, video games, ISRO simulations) are displayed on 2D screens. (3) Manufacturing: every product is designed on 2D paper/screen before being built in 3D. (4) Medical imaging: MRI and CT scans show 2D cross-sections of 3D body structures. (5) Geography: maps are 2D representations of 3D terrain. The ability to mentally flip between 2D and 3D is one of the most valuable spatial skills.",memory_hook:"2D\u21923D skill = the SUPERPOWER of engineers, architects, doctors, and game designers! Flat drawing \u2192 real building. Flat map \u2192 real terrain. 2D screen \u2192 3D game world. Every professional who BUILDS things uses this skill daily."},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"If you cut a cube in half horizontally, what shape would you see? If you cut it diagonally (corner to corner), what shape?",back:"Horizontal cut (parallel to base): the cross-section is a SQUARE (same as the base of the cube). Diagonal cut from edge to edge through the centre: the cross-section is a RECTANGLE. Diagonal cut from corner to corner (through opposite vertices): the cross-section is a RECTANGLE (specifically an oblong rectangle). Diagonal cut from four midpoints of edges: produces a regular HEXAGON cross-section! This is a fascinating result \u2014 slicing a cube can reveal squares, rectangles, or hexagons depending on the angle of cut.",memory_hook:"CUBE cross-sections: Horizontal = SQUARE. Diagonal = RECTANGLE. Special diagonal through edge midpoints = HEXAGON! Most surprising: a cube can hide a hexagon inside! This is why 3D visualisation is both useful AND surprising \u2014 even simple shapes have hidden complexity."}]}},2967,[]);